# TaskPump's own run state. These are created in the root of whatever repo the
# pump drives; they are listed here too because TaskPump is itself a repo a pump
# can be pointed at, and because the contamination guard's allowlist and this
# file have to agree — a run file that is tracked here but allowlisted there is
# a dirty tree nobody notices, and one that is neither is a warning every tick.
.taskpump-pump.state
.taskpump-pump.log
.taskpump-pool-cap
.taskpump-usage-reset
.taskpump-fsguard.notified
.taskpump-disk-watchdog.log
.taskpump-task.lock
# The historical spellings stay: a pump pointed here under the Arachne
# reference pins (examples/arachne.conf) writes these names instead.
.arachne-pump.state
.arachne-pump.log
.arachne-pool-cap
.arachne-usage-reset
.arachne-fsguard.notified
.arachne-disk-watchdog.log
.arachne-agent-watchdog.log
.arachne-task.lock
.auto-trunk.lock
.auto-trunk-quarantine
# Worktrees must be VISIBLE to git: the pump refuses to launch into a
# gitignored worktree (tp-pump's check-ignore guard — the 2026-04-14 incident),
# and the operator's global excludes (~/.config/git/ignore) ignores
# **/.worktrees/** in every repo. A repo-level negation outranks the global
# source, so this line is what lets the dogfood pump launch at all; fs-guard's
# default allowlist (^\.worktrees/) is what tolerates the untracked dirt this
# re-exposes. Do NOT add a bare `.worktrees/` ignore here — the pump's
# gitignore-repair hook deletes it on every tick (that is the incident).
!.worktrees/**
